{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01N6EbtE8hTovbfESDRgaFZ6","type":"tool_result","content":"<system-reminder>[Truncated: PARTIAL view — showing lines 1-862 of 1555 total (38323 tokens, cap 25000). Call Read with offset=863 limit=862 for the next page, or Grep to find a specific section. Do NOT answer from this page alone if the answer may be further in the file.]</system-reminder>\n\n1\t<!-- Copied verbatim on 2026-08-30 from C:/Projects/ai-template-plans/complexity-pushback/PLAN.md — the plan of record for the rows that cite it; its companions there (the source reports, the prototype, the drafts, the review transcripts) stay outside the repo. -->\n2\t\n3\t# complexity-pushback — repo-integration plan\n4\t\n5\t**Status:** plan only. Nothing in `C:\\Projects\\ai-template` was modified writing this.\n6\t**Written:** 2026-08-29. **Read at:** `ai-template` HEAD `85fb3742` (branch `contract_split`).\n7\t**Working tree at survey time was dirty in four paths** — `PROJECT_STATE.html`,\n8\t`docs/decisions-for-review-2026-08-29-slices-4-6.md`, `docs/stage`, `docs/status.md`,\n9\tplus an untracked `docs/log.d/2026-08-29-owner-rulings-oi67-decisions.md`. Everything quoted\n10\tbelow was read from the working tree EXCEPT `docs/status.md`, which was read at HEAD via\n11\t`git show HEAD:docs/status.md`. A session executing this plan must re-read those five paths\n12\tbefore touching them: another session was mid-OI-67-slice-6 the whole time.\n13\t\n14\t**Re-verified after the concurrent session committed.** While this plan was being written the\n15\ttree advanced to `b19d4bf7` (\"sitting: the owner accepts decisions 4.1, 6.2, 6.7 and 6.8 of the\n16\tOI-67 slices — recorded, nothing changes in code\") and went clean. That commit touched only\n17\t`PROJECT_STATE.html`, `docs/stage`, `docs/status.md`, `docs/decisions-for-review-2026-08-29-slices-4-6.md`\n18\tand a new log fragment — none of which this plan quotes normatively. **Every load-bearing\n19\tnumber below was re-measured at `b19d4bf7` and is unchanged:** the watermark still reads\n20\t`IF=173 LLR=205 OI=67 SR=182 TC=201 WI=534`, and `wc -c` still gives `PROCESS.md` 87,836 ·\n21\t`PROCESS_OPTIONS.md` 179,209 · `AGENTS.template.md` 9,980 · `byte-budget-guard/SKILL.md` 4,795.\n22\t\n23\t**Sources this plan executes:** `..\\knowledge-pack-review\\00-SYNTHESIS.md` §2, §3 ranks 1–3,\n24\t§5 plan 1; `..\\knowledge-pack-review\\05-compact-code-and-complexity-pushback.md` §2, §4\n25\t(P1/P2/P3/P4/P6), §5. **The owner has approved the proposal in principle**; phase 0 is the\n26\truling that decides its shape, not its existence.\n27\t\n28\t**The script itself is not in this plan.** A sibling agent is prototyping it under\n29\t`prototype/` — it now exists (`prototype/check_complexity.py`, 49 tests green, census in\n30\t`prototype/analysis.txt` and `prototype/PROTOTYPE-NOTES.md`; see `README.md` beside this file).\n31\tThe prototype's numbers are the WORKING figures in this plan and the OI brief. Do not copy a\n32\tnumber out of report 05 into a registry cell or a baseline file: report 05's figures were taken\n33\twith an unvalidated walker and read materially high (`plan_round.record` 135 there, 61 by the\n34\tvalidated prototype). The shipped script's own first run is the only number that may be stamped\n35\tinto the repo; the prototype's are what the ruling is read against.\n36\t\n37\t---\n38\t\n39\t## 0. What the program is, in one paragraph\n40\t\n41\tBuild `check_complexity.py` — a stdlib-`ast` Sonar-style **cognitive complexity** + SLOC\n42\tcensus per function (plus a reported, ungated public-symbol count per module), with a central\n43\tTSV baseline, exact equality in both directions, `--report` / `--restamp`, and **no inline\n44\tsuppression pragma ever**. Ship it `--report`-only; arming it is an opt-in\n45\t`PROCESS_OPTIONS.md` layer. Land it **as a trade, not an addition**: the same ruling that\n46\tadmits it decides whether `tests/test_module_size_ratchet.py` retires, which is the owner\n47\tquestion already banked in the 2026-08-20 log. Alongside it: a `deep-module-design`\n48\t`scope: kit` skill (the one real content gap), and one PROCESS.md §3 bullet formalising the\n49\tstructure-vs-behaviour commit split that WI-521 slices 1–2 already practised.\n50\t\n51\t**Two rulings constrain everything here and neither is reopened.** **D-7** (2026-08-10) tore\n52\tdown the *gating* duplication census; any proposal that re-arms a duplication gate is dead on\n53\tarrival, and this program adds no duplication check of any kind. **OI-16** (owner correction,\n54\t2026-08-12) is the program's warrant: *\"the monolith risk was really around function size /\n55\tcomplexity, not strictly file size.\"*\n56\t\n57\t---\n58\t\n59\t## 1. The seams, exactly as they are today\n60\t\n61\tEach subsection records what the repo does **now**, with the file path and a verbatim\n62\tpattern. This is the reference the execution WIs work from; nothing here is a proposal.\n63\t\n64\t### 1.1 OI filing — `docs/requirements/open-items.toml`\n65\t\n66\t**Carrier.** One TOML table per decision, id-keyed and bare: `[open_item.OI-###]`. The file's\n67\town header states the contract (it declares `IF-073`):\n68\t\n69\t```\n70\t# Contract IF-073: one `[open_item.OI-###]` table per owner decision, carrying\n71\t#     `title`, `status`, `raised`, `one_line`, `decision`, `blast_radius`,\n72\t#     `options`, `recommendation` and `wi_refs`. `status` is `pending` or\n73\t#     `ruled`, and only a pending row renders as a brief card — a ruled row is\n74\t#     history, and the Decisions log is its record. `-000` example rows are\n75\t#     dropped, and an absent registry renders an empty queue rather than\n76\t#     failing, so a repo carrying no decisions still has the surface.\n77\t```\n78\t\n79\t**The `-000` row IS the schema** (`docs/requirements/open-items.toml` lines 16–27), and its\n80\tcell values are the authoring guidance. Copied verbatim as the pattern to fill:\n81\t\n82\t```toml\n83\t[open_item.OI-000]\n84\ttitle = \"Example row - the shape of an owner decision brief\"\n85\tstatus = \"pending\"\n86\traised = \"2026-01-01\"\n87\tone_line = \"\"\"the one-line form status.md projects - what is being decided, and the recommendation in a clause\"\"\"\n88\tdecision = \"\"\"What is being decided, who raised it and when, and any context a ruler needs that is not obvious from the one-line.\"\"\"\n89\tblast_radius = \"\"\"What breaks or changes if this is ruled either way - the honest cost, not the optimistic one.\"\"\"\n90\toptions = \"\"\"Option (a) ... · Option (b) ... · Option (c) ... - each with its FOR and AGAINST, separated by the middot the other registries use for lists-in-cells.\"\"\"\n91\trecommendation = \"The driver's recommendation and WHY, in the same voice a reviewer would argue it.\"\n92\twi_refs = [\"WI-000\"]\n93\t```\n94\t\n95\t**Field census over the 59 real rows** (`grep -oE '^[a-z_]+ = ' | sort | uniq -c`):\n96\t`title`/`status`/`recommendation`/`raised`/`options`/`one_line` on all 59; `ruling_ref` and\n97\t`ruled_date` on 58; `blast_radius` on 57; `decision` on 49; `wi_refs` on 26. So\n98\t`blast_radius` and `decision` are conventionally-required-but-not-schema-required, and\n99\t`wi_refs` is genuinely optional — **a brief may be filed with no WI row yet**, which is what\n100\tphase 0 does.\n101\t\n102\t**How the fields actually work, read from live rows.**\n103\t\n104\t- `status` — `pending` while it owes a ruling, flipped to `ruled` when the owner answers.\n105\t  Only a `pending` row renders as a card. **There is exactly one `status = \"pending\"` row in\n106\t  the file today and it is `OI-000`, the example** — every real OI is ruled. So the phase-0\n107\t  brief will be the only live card, which is the surface the owner reads.\n108\t- `raised` — the date the question was put, `YYYY-MM-DD`.\n109\t- `wi_refs` — `[\"WI-###\"]`, the rows that carry the work. Filled after the ruling for a\n110\t  brief filed before any WI exists.\n111\t- `one_line` — what `docs/status.md` projects. **The house convention on a ruled row is to\n112\t  PREPEND the ruling and keep the original one-line behind it**, e.g. OI-67:\n113\t  `\"\"\"RULED (a) 2026-08-29 — the owner: \"...\" Original brief: rule the shape of an interface row; recommend (a): ...\"\"\"`\n114\t- `decision` — what is being decided plus the context a ruler needs. On a ruled row the\n115\t  ruling is written INTO this cell at the top (OI-16 opens `RULED ADDENDUM (owner,\n116\t  2026-08-13): ...`).\n117\t- `ruled_date` + `ruling_ref` — added at the ruling. `ruling_ref` points at\n118\t  `docs/log.md#decisions-log`, `docs/repo-lock.md#d-5`, or a\n119\t  `docs/log.d/<date>-<slug>.md` fragment (OI-67 uses\n120\t  `docs/log.d/2026-08-29-oi67-ruled-a.md`).\n121\t- `options` — lettered, each with FOR and AGAINST. Long rows use `###`-headed markdown\n122\t  inside the `\"\"\"` string (OI-67 does); short rows use one `·`-separated line (OI-11 does).\n123\t- `recommendation` — the driver's call and why, argued in a reviewer's voice.\n124\t\n125\t**How the brief is composed and rendered.** There is no composer script — the brief is\n126\t**hand-authored into the TOML row**, and `project-trajectory/scripts/gen_open_items.py`\n127\tRENDERS it. That module's docstring is explicit about owning no second opinion:\n128\t\n129\t> ANTI-DUPLICATION, deliberately: the git archaeology and the cell comparison live in\n130\t> `trace.reattest_model`, and the pending projection lives in `pending.pending_block`. This\n131\t> module imports both and RENDERS.\n132\t\n133\tIt emits three ordered sections into `docs/open-items.html`: (1) one card per\n134\t`status=pending` row (the one-line, what is being decided, blast radius, options,\n135\trecommendation, and the WI rows that carry the work); (2) approval & re-attestation, every\n136\t`Drafted`-or-drifted SR with its whole chain; (3) pending owner actions, reused verbatim from\n137\t`pending.pending_block`.\n138\t\n139\t**Regen command** (`--root` defaults to cwd, `--check` byte-compares for the freshness gate;\n140\tthe step name is `open-items` in `check.py` and the `[generated]` row is\n141\t`docs/open-items.html = openitems`):\n142\t\n143\t```\n144\tpython project-trajectory/scripts/gen_open_items.py --root .\n145\tpython project-trajectory/scripts/gen_open_items.py --root . --check   # the freshness gate\n146\t```\n147\t\n148\t**`adjudicate_brief.py` is a different machine — do not reach for it here.** It fills an\n149\t*adjudication session's* prompt for a WI row carrying `safety_class = \"adjudication\"` and a\n150\tdeclared `brief` key (`amendment|disposition|conflict|red-tc`). It has nothing to do with an\n151\topen-item brief. Its two governing rules are worth knowing anyway because they bear on how\n152\tthis program's evidence is presented: *\"a judge's brief never contains the claim under\n153\tjudgement as its premise\"*, and *\"a half-filled brief is WORSE than the generic prompt — a\n154\tbrief whose evidence section is thin does not fail loudly, it reads as a completed\n155\tinvestigation that found nothing\"* — which is why the OI draft below marks every unfilled\n156\tnumber `<<prototype>>` instead of guessing one.\n157\t\n158\t**What OI-16 already rules, and what it leaves open.** OI-16 (`status = \"ruled\"`,\n159\t`ruled_date = \"2026-08-13\"`, `wi_refs = [\"WI-448\"]`) is the common-module doctrine. Two of its\n160\tcells carry the part that matters here. The `decision` cell:\n161\t\n162\t> The owner also corrected the reasoning this brief had used against a single large module:\n163\t> big files are not inherently a problem, and the monolith risk was always about FUNCTION\n164\t> size and complexity, not file length. That correction is accepted and has a consequence of\n165\t> its own, in the last bullet below.\n166\t\n167\tand the last bullet of `blast_radius`:\n168\t\n169\t> - The ratchet: the module-size ratchet measures LINES, so a consolidated common file will\n170\t>   fire it. By the owner's own correction that is the wrong axis — it should measure\n171\t>   function size and complexity. Bumping a line baseline for a file that got SIMPLER is the\n172\t>   check asking for the opposite of what is wanted, and it is now the second reason to\n173\t>   revisit that ratchet.\n174\t\n175\t**So OI-16 rules the AXIS and explicitly does NOT rule the retirement.** The retirement is\n176\tthe *banked question*, and it is banked in two homes, both verbatim below.\n177\t\n178\t**Home 1 — `docs/log.d/2026-08-20-program-grind.md` lines 2174–2196** (the 2026-08-20 log the\n179\tratchet's docstring points at):\n180\t\n181\t> - **The module-size ratchet measures the wrong axis, and WI-448 is the second witness the\n182\t>   owner's own correction predicted** (OI-16: \"the monolith risk was always about FUNCTION\n183\t>   size and complexity, not file length\"). Concretely: `bootstrap.py` shed two duplicated\n184\t>   helper BODIES and gained a MAPPING declaration block, and the line ratchet demanded a\n185\t>   reviewed bump on a file that got structurally simpler; meanwhile\n186\t>   `agent_common`/`check_trajectory` each lost 230-ish lines of pure duplication and the\n187\t>   ratchet's only response was \"re-stamp downward\". Neither direction told anyone anything\n188\t>   about complexity. Note the axis it wants ALREADY EXISTS unused-for-this-purpose:\n189\t>   `tests/test_complexity_ratchet.py` runs ruff `C901` per function and, unlike the line\n190\t>   ratchet, RECURSES into packages. NOT redesigned here, deliberately — filing it is the\n191\t>   WI's instruction. **The question for the owner is whether the line ratchet retires in\n192\t>   favour of the complexity one, or the two keep different jobs with the line one demoted to\n193\t>   advisory.**\n194\t> - **The line ratchet is blind to packages.** `test_module_size_ratchet._census` globs\n195\t>   `scripts/*.py` (top-level only), so every module under `scripts/kitlib/` is uncensused —\n196\t>   a 3,000-line module could land there and the ratchet would never see it. Not fixed in\n197\t>   WI-448 because the fix forces a keying decision (`path.name` collides once two packages\n198\t>   hold a `registry.py`; `test_complexity_ratchet` already keys on the relpath) and that is\n199\t>   the same axis question above. Cheap to fix, but it should be fixed WITH the ruling, not\n200\t>   before it.\n201\t\n202\t(The package-blindness half was since fixed — the ratchet's own docstring now says it\n203\t\"recurses into packages too since 2026-08-21 (see `_all_modules`)\" — which **strengthens** the\n204\ttrade: *\"the two now cover the same files and differ only in what they measure about them.\"*)\n205\t\n206\t**Home 2 — `tests/test_module_size_ratchet.py`, the closing lines of its module docstring:**\n207\t\n208\t> AND THE SENSOR MEASURES AN AXIS ITS OWN OWNER DISPUTES. The owner's `OI-16` correction is\n209\t> that \"the monolith risk was always about FUNCTION size and complexity, not file length\",\n210\t> and WI-448 produced the worked counterexample: `bootstrap.py` shed two duplicated helper\n211\t> bodies, gained a MAPPING declaration block, and this file demanded a reviewed bump on a\n212\t> module that got structurally simpler. `tests/test_complexity_ratchet.py` measures the\n213\t> disputed axis; this census recurses into packages too since 2026-08-21 (see\n214\t> `_all_modules`), so the two now cover the same files and differ only in what they measure\n215\t> about them. Whether the line ratchet retires in favour of the complexity one or the two\n216\t> keep different jobs is an open question for the owner, banked in the 2026-08-20 log; until\n217\t> it is ruled, this file stays armed, because an unruled axis is not a reason to stop sensing\n218\t> growth.\n219\t\n220\t### 1.2 WI filing — `docs/work/`\n221\t\n222\t**Status is the DIRECTORY**, never a frontmatter field. `docs/work/README.md` declares five\n223\tseams (`IF-023`, `IF-024`, `IF-054`, `IF-079`, `IF-159`) in an HTML-comment header; the three\n224\tthis program touches, verbatim:\n225\t\n226\t- **`IF-023`** — the registry as a validated registry: \"One row is one Markdown spec file\n227\t  whose `+++` frontmatter carries `id`, `title`, `workstream`, `sr_refs`, `needs` and the\n228\t  optional scheduling keys, and whose `## Deliverable` body is the backward-only record; the\n229\t  filename repeats the id. STATUS IS THE DIRECTORY, so a spec under a directory outside the\n230\t  declared set RAISES rather than being skipped — inventing a folder takes rows out of the\n231\t  registry instead of adding a state to it.\"\n232\t- **`IF-054`** — the same registry read for READINESS: \"A row offers its status, its `needs`\n233\t  edges (an entry prefixed `~` is a SOFT edge and never blocks) and the optional `priority`,\n234\t  `exclusive`, `blockref`, `est_tokens` and `safety_class` keys. An absent optional key reads\n235\t  as its documented default, and the defaults are chosen to fail closed: an absent safety\n236\t  class is `unclassified` and is never scheduled... **A hard edge is satisfied only by an\n237\t  integrated `done` predecessor** — a cancelled one never satisfies it.\"\n238\t- **`IF-159`** — the WRITE side and the format's single writer: \"one registry row rendered as\n239\t  one spec file under the row's status directory, the filename repeating the id, UTF-8 with\n240\t  LF endings on every platform. The file is RE-READ and re-parsed before its path is\n241\t  returned: a spec whose frontmatter does not reconstruct its source row cell-exact, the\n242\t  order key included, raises.\"\n243\t\n244\t**The writer named by IF-159 is `project-trajectory/scripts/wi_convert.py`.** Row of record in\n245\t`docs/requirements/interfaces.toml`:\n246\t\n247\t```toml\n248\t[interface.IF-159]\n249\towner = \"docs/work/\"\n250\trequestors = [\"scripts/wi_convert\"]\n251\tchannel = \"file\"\n252\tdata = \"one spec file per row, written and read back cell-exact before its path is returned\"\n253\tversion = \"v1\"\n254\tstatus = \"Drafted\"\n255\tcomponent = \"CMP-006\"\n256\t```\n257\t\n258\tThe function is `wi_convert.write_spec_file(work_dir, row, order=None)` (line 552).\n259\t`intake.py` is what MINTS (`_mint`, `_mint_shape_refusal`, `mint_gap_rows`) and drives the\n260\twriter.\n261\t\n262\t**The exemplar, `docs/work/queued/WI-000-example.md`** — frontmatter verbatim:\n263\t\n264\t```\n265\t+++\n266\tid = \"WI-000\"\n267\ttitle = \"EXAMPLE work item (permanent exemplar — keep)\"\n268\tworkstream = \"example\"\n269\tsr_refs = []\n270\tneeds = []\n271\tbuildtier = \"medium\"\n272\tpriority = 0\n273\t+++\n274\t```\n275\t\n276\tIts own key documentation, condensed: all keys optional except `id` and `title`; **an absent\n277\tkey and an empty one mean the same thing, so empty keys are omitted rather than written as a\n278\twall of `= \"\"`**. `workstream` = the mutable grouping. `sr_refs` = the requirement ids it\n279\tdelivers. `needs` = predecessor ids; a bare id is a HARD edge, a `~`-prefixed id is a SOFT\n280\tedge, \"the `~` is meaning, not decoration, and is carried verbatim\". `specref` = the forward\n281\tbridge (rule R-E), a `docs/specs/WI-###.md` file or a `doc#anchor` that resolves while open\n282\tand clears at close. **`buildtier` is the frontmatter spelling** (`strong|medium|quick`;\n283\tlegacy `weak` reads as `quick`; \"the STARTING tier for this item's build sessions and never\n284\tcaps escalation\") — `BuildTier` is the registry-column spelling, not the key. `priority` int\n285\tdefault 0; `exclusive` semantic mutex keys; `blockref` (its PRESENCE is what makes a `queued`\n286\trow `blocked`); `est_tokens`; `safety_class` ∈\n287\t`ordinary|spine|gate|attestation|protected|high-risk|adjudication`, **fails closed** (absent\n288\treads `unclassified` and is never scheduled); `planmode = \"dual\"`; `brief` only on an\n289\tadjudication row; `order` is a migration artifact a hand-filed spec omits.\n290\t\n291\t**The body while a row is OPEN.** `## Deliverable` is **backward-only (rule R-A)**: it stays\n292\tEMPTY while the work is open and is filled with what shipped at close; `check_trajectory`'s\n293\tR-A is a hard finding both ways (a non-terminal WI with a filled Deliverable, or a terminal\n294\tWI with an empty one). The forward-looking narrative goes in a `## Context` section, which\n295\t`kitlib/registry.py::parse_spec_deliverable` clips off before reading the cell\n296\t(`SPEC_CONTEXT = \"\\n## Context\\n\"`, lines 156–162: \"so a minted row whose body is\n297\tcontext-only parses with an empty Deliverable rather than as a malformation\").\n298\t**`docs/work/active/wi521-decomposition-debt-owner/WI-521-decomposition-debt-owner.md` is the\n299\tworked example of that shape** — `## Context` with `###` sub-sections and per-slice landing\n300\trecords, and no `## Deliverable` at all. Every WI draft in §2 follows it.\n301\t\n302\t**How ids are minted, and the rule a lane must obey.** Owner ruling **R1** (2026-08-01,\n303\texecuted as WI-397, `docs/log.md` §\"2026-08-01 — WI-397: a work branch never mints a\n304\twork-item id\"):\n305\t\n306\t> Minting a work-item id is a **serial trunk-side act only**. A new work item takes\n307\t> `max(existing id) + 1`, a lane can only see its own tree, and the arithmetic does the rest\n308\t> — on 2026-08-01 two lanes independently minted `WI-392`, and three rows lived only on the\n309\t> unmerged `wi-391` branch while trunk's max sat *below* them... An id a branch cannot create\n310\t> cannot collide.\n311\t\n312\tEnforced by `integrate._minted_id_refusal`: at the merge slot, a finished branch whose\n313\t`docs/work/` delta from `merge-base(trunk, branch)` to tip **ADDS** a spec file carrying an id\n314\toutside its claimed set is refused, naming the foreign ids, the paths, the claimed set and the\n315\trule. Adds-only, spec-filenames-only, `--no-renames` load-bearing. The authoritative counter\n316\tis `docs/id-watermark` (generated by `trace.py --bump-ids`, never hand-edited; a mark only\n317\tever RISES, and lowering one is refused by the integrity pass).\n318\t\n319\t**Watermark at HEAD `85fb3742`** — so the next mints are the numbers below **if nothing else\n320\tmints first**; the executing session must re-read the file, never trust this:\n321\t\n322\t```\n323\tbasis: B=5 CMP=9 EXT=5 IF=173 LLR=205 OI=67 PB=4 REL=3 SN=40 SR=182 TC=201 WI=534\n324\t```\n325\t\n326\t→ next: **OI-68, WI-535, SR-183, LLR-206, TC-202, IF-174**.\n327\t\n328\t### 1.3 WI-521 — extend it, or sit beside it?\n329\t\n330\t**Read in full.** `WI-521` is `active` under\n331\t`docs/work/active/wi521-decomposition-debt-owner/`, `buildtier = \"strong\"`,\n332\t`safety_class = \"ordinary\"`, `priority = 2`, `sr_refs = []`, `needs = []`,\n333\t`specref = \"docs/plans/2026-08-25-remap-alignment.md\"`. It is\n334\t**`tests/test_module_size_ratchet.py`'s named debt owner**, and it says of itself:\n335\t\n336\t> **THIS ROW IS A STANDING DEBT OWNER, NOT A ONE-SITTING TASK.** Do not claim it expecting to\n337\t> finish it. It is claimable for one scoped slice at a time, and it is closed only when the\n338\t> debt below is paid or re-homed — and if it is ever closed, **the ratchet pointer must move\n339\t> in the same commit**, which is the rule it inherited.\n340\t\n341\tIt owns three things: (1) the four wide modules (`agent_loop`, `check_trajectory`,\n342\t`agent_common`, `bootstrap`), corroborated from the requirements side by two independent\n343\tderivations; (2) M-06's four test monoliths (slice 2 split `test_integrate.py` by node-id set\n344\tequality; `test_trace.py` 2,099, `test_trajectory_arch.py` 1,993, `test_agent_loop.py` 1,640\n345\tremain); (3) **the sensor gap** — and its §3 is the load-bearing paragraph for this decision:\n346\t\n347\t> `tests/test_module_size_ratchet.py` censuses `SCRIPTS` only, so **no armed sensor watches\n348\t> the test tree** — which is why three of the four monoliths grew 5–36% between the\n349\t> 2026-08-19 review and the `WI-483` close with nothing saying so.\n350\t>\n351\t> **Do not just extend the census.** That file's own docstring banks an unruled owner\n352\t> question — whether the line-count axis survives at all, given the owner's `OI-16`\n353\t> correction... Extending a disputed axis to a second tree doubles whatever is wrong with it.\n354\t> **The honest sequence is to raise the axis question with the measurement this row can now\n355\t> supply, and extend only what survives the answer.**\n356\t\n357\tSlice 2's close restates it: *\"the 
